{"title":"Email Overload: Exploring Personal Information Management of Email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/238386.238530","doi":"10.1145/238386.238530","authors":["Steve Whittaker","Candace Sidner"],"year":1996,"published":"1996-04-13","abstract":"Empirical workplace-email research showing that email had become a medium for task management and personal archiving as well as communication. Users' inboxes contained outstanding tasks, partially read documents, and conversation threads, and important messages could be overlooked or lost in archives.","venue":"CHI 1996"}
{"title":"Advantages of Query Biased Summaries in Information Retrieval","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/290941.290947","doi":"10.1145/290941.290947","authors":["Anastasios Tombros","Mark Sanderson"],"year":1998,"abstract":"Investigates query-biased, user-directed summaries customized to an information need and evaluates whether they help users make relevance judgments more quickly and accurately.","venue":"SIGIR 1998"}
{"title":"The Use of MMR, Diversity-Based Reranking for Reordering Documents and Producing Summaries","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/290941.291025","doi":"10.1145/290941.291025","authors":["Jaime G. Carbonell","Jade Goldstein"],"year":1998,"abstract":"Introduces Maximal Marginal Relevance, which combines query relevance with information novelty to reduce redundancy in retrieval and summarization. The method is particularly applicable to selecting non-redundant content from multiple documents.","venue":"SIGIR 1998"}
{"title":"On-Line New Event Detection and Tracking","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/290941.290954","doi":"10.1145/290941.290954","authors":["James Allan","Ron Papka","Victor Lavrenko"],"year":1998,"abstract":"Studies online detection of previously unseen events and tracking of continuing events in a stream of documents, providing an early foundation for distinguishing newly arriving information from previously observed material.","venue":"SIGIR 1998"}
{"title":"Information Fusion in the Context of Multi-Document Summarization","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P99-1071/","doi":"10.3115/1034678.1034760","authors":["Regina Barzilay","Kathleen R. McKeown","Michael Elhadad"],"year":1999,"published":"1999-06-20","abstract":"Presents a method for producing concise summaries by identifying and synthesizing related or similar information across multiple documents.","venue":"ACL 1999"}
{"title":"Reinventing the inbox: supporting the management of pending tasks in email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/506443.506476","doi":"10.1145/506443.506476","authors":["Jacek Gwizdka"],"year":2002,"abstract":"Examines email as a tool for managing pending tasks and explores external representations of messages and their associated tasks to make outstanding work easier to manage.","venue":"CHI 2002 Extended Abstracts"}
{"title":"TaskView: Design and Evaluation of a Task-Based Email Interface","url":"https://eprints.rclis.org/13700/","doi":"10.1145/782115.782119","authors":["Jacek Gwizdka"],"year":2002,"abstract":"Studies support for managing multiple pending tasks in email through alternative external representations. An evaluation compares a conventional inbox with TimeStore-TaskView for finding task dates, temporal information, task overviews, and message information.","venue":"CASCON 2002"}
{"title":"Taking Email to Task: The Design and Evaluation of a Task Management Centered Email Tool","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/642611.642672","doi":"10.1145/642611.642672","authors":["Victoria Bellotti","Nicolas Ducheneaut","Mark Howard","Ian Smith"],"year":2003,"published":"2003-04-05","abstract":"Describes Taskmaster, which recasts email around task management and embeds task-centric resources in the email client. Field research and studies using live email examine the value of this task-centered design for overloaded users.","venue":"CHI 2003"}
{"title":"Email Task Management Styles: The Cleaners and the Keepers","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/985921.986032","doi":"10.1145/985921.986032","authors":["Jacek Gwizdka"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Examines individual differences in how people use email to manage messages associated with future tasks and events, identifying contrasting task-management styles with implications for personalized support.","venue":"CHI 2004 Extended Abstracts"}
{"title":"Individual differences and task-based user interface evaluation: a case study of pending tasks in email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.intcom.2004.04.008","doi":"10.1016/j.intcom.2004.04.008","authors":["Jacek Gwizdka","Mark Chignell"],"year":2004,"published":"2004-05-28","abstract":"Evaluates representations intended to make pending email tasks more visible and examines how their effectiveness interacts with individual cognitive differences. The study compares a task-oriented email representation with a conventional inbox.","venue":"Interacting with Computers"}
{"title":"What a to-do: studies of task management towards the design of a personal task list manager","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/985692.985785","doi":"10.1145/985692.985785","authors":["Victoria Bellotti","Brinda Dalal","Nathaniel Good","Peter Flynn","Daniel G. Bobrow","Nicolas Ducheneaut"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Studies how people organize and complete to-do items across media. The work identifies nuanced prioritization strategies and factors influencing task priority and completion, informing the design of personal task-management systems.","venue":"CHI 2004"}
{"title":"Task-Focused Summarization of Email","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W04-1008/","authors":["Simon Corston-Oliver","Eric Ringger","Michael Gamon","Richard Campbell"],"year":2004,"abstract":"Describes SmartMail, which automatically identifies action items in email and presents them as a task-focused summary, including support for transferring identified actions into a to-do list.","venue":"Text Summarization Branches Out 2004"}
{"title":"Supporting Collaborative Task Management in E-mail","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/07370024.2005.9667361","doi":"10.1207/s15327051hci2001&2_3","authors":["Steve Whittaker"],"year":2005,"abstract":"Examines collaborative tasks conducted through email, where interactions may extend over time and users must track multiple outstanding tasks while awaiting responses. The paper discusses reminders, grouping related communications, and combining task-relevant information in support tools.","venue":"Human–Computer Interaction"}
{"title":"Quality Versus Quantity: E-Mail-Centric Task Management and Its Relation With Overload","url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/07370024.2005.9667362","doi":"10.1207/s15327051hci2001&2_4","authors":["Victoria Bellotti","Nicolas Ducheneaut","Mark Howard","Ian Smith","Rebecca E. Grinter"],"year":2005,"abstract":"Fieldwork identifies task-management challenges associated with email, including collaborative tasks that remain incomplete while users wait for responses and therefore require continued tracking. The paper also evaluates the TaskMaster approach using real email.","venue":"Human–Computer Interaction"}
{"title":"Beyond “From” and “Received”: Exploring the Dynamics of Email Triage","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1056808.1057071","doi":"10.1145/1056808.1057071","authors":["Carman Neustaedter","A. J. Bernheim Brush","Marc A. Smith"],"year":2005,"abstract":"Studies how people triage email and what information helps them decide which messages deserve attention. The work examines socially salient cues that can help surface important messages.","venue":"CHI 2005 Extended Abstracts"}
{"title":"Understanding email use: predicting action on a message","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1054972.1055068","doi":"10.1145/1054972.1055068","authors":["Laura A. Dabbish","Robert E. Kraut","Susan R. Fussell","Sara B. Kiesler"],"year":2005,"abstract":"An organizational study examines ratings of message importance and the actions recipients take on incoming email. Messages were judged important particularly when they concerned work and required action, while actual handling also depended on personal tendencies, work demands, and relationships.","venue":"CHI 2005"}
{"title":"Going with the flow: email awareness and task management","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1180875.1180942","doi":"10.1145/1180875.1180942","authors":["Nelson Siu","Lee Iverson","Anthony Tang"],"year":2006,"abstract":"Studies how workers interleave email with ongoing day-to-day work. The findings show email being used to manage moment-to-moment attention and task focus and motivate workflow-oriented email-client design.","venue":"CSCW 2006"}
{"title":"Detecting Action Items in Multi-party Meetings: Annotation and Initial Experiments","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/11965152_18","doi":"10.1007/11965152_18","authors":["Matthew Purver","Patrick Ehlen","John Niekrasz"],"year":2006,"abstract":"Introduces an annotation framework for action items in multi-party meetings and experiments with detecting the dialogue associated with task assignment. The representation captures roles played by utterances in establishing future actions.","venue":"Machine Learning for Multimodal Interaction"}
{"title":"Can requests-for-action and commitments-to-act be reliably identified in email messages?","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/3013952.3013959","authors":["Andrew Lampert","Cécile Paris","Robert Dale"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Investigates whether requests for action and commitments to act can be reliably annotated in workplace email. These communicative acts provide explicit evidence of obligations and promised future work rather than relying on reply behavior alone.","venue":"Australasian Document Computing Symposium 2007"}
{"title":"Summarizing Email Conversations with Clue Words","url":"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~carenini/PAPERS/www07.pdf","authors":["Giuseppe Carenini","Raymond T. Ng","Xiaodong Zhou"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Presents an email-conversation summarization approach that models quotation and reply relationships between message fragments and uses clue words to identify salient content across a thread.","venue":"WWW 2007"}
{"title":"Detecting and Summarizing Action Items in Multi-Party Dialogue","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2007.sigdial-1.4/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2007.sigdial-1.4","authors":["Matthew Purver","John Dowding","John Niekrasz","Patrick Ehlen","Sharareh Noorbaloochi","Stanley Peters"],"year":2007,"abstract":"Detects dialogue regions where action items are proposed, discussed, and committed to, then extracts phrases describing properties of the resulting tasks. The method uses dialogue structure and semantic information to support task-oriented summaries.","venue":"SIGDIAL 2007"}
{"title":"The nature of requests and commitments in email messages","url":"https://researchers.mq.edu.au/en/publications/the-nature-of-requests-and-commitments-in-email-messages/","authors":["Andrew Lampert","Robert Dale","Cécile Paris"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Develops definitions and an annotation scheme for requests and commitments in workplace email. Requests can create obligations for other participants, while commitments record obligations voluntarily undertaken, with representations that include actions, actors, and optional conditions.","venue":"AAAI Workshop 2008"}
{"title":"Summarizing Emails with Conversational Cohesion and Subjectivity","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P08-1041/","authors":["Giuseppe Carenini","Raymond T. Ng","Xiaodong Zhou"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Develops email-conversation summarization methods that exploit conversational cohesion, including quotation structure, together with subjective information to select salient material from email threads.","venue":"ACL 2008"}
{"title":"Exploiting Dialog Act Tagging and Prosodic Information for Action Item Identification","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICASSP.2008.4518766","doi":"10.1109/ICASSP.2008.4518766","authors":["Fan Yang","Gokhan Tur","Elizabeth Shriberg"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Investigates identification of meeting action items, defined as tasks agreed for execution after a meeting, including descriptions, owners, or due-date information. Dialog-act and prosodic features are used to identify action descriptions and agreement signals.","venue":"ICASSP 2008"}
{"title":"Leveraging Minimal User Input to Improve Targeted Extraction of Action Items","url":"http://semdial.org/anthology/Z08-Frampton_semdial_0019.pdf","authors":["Matthew Frampton","Raquel Fernández","Patrick Ehlen","Anish Adukuzhiyil","Stanley Peters"],"year":2008,"abstract":"Studies action-item extraction aided by a low-effort signal from meeting participants indicating when an action item is being discussed. The approach illustrates how explicit user input can focus extraction on information the user considers operationally relevant.","venue":"SemDial 2008"}
{"title":"Mining social networks for personalized email prioritization","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/1557019.1557124","doi":"10.1145/1557019.1557124","authors":["Shinjae Yoo","Yiming Yang","Frank Lin","Il-Chul Moon"],"year":2009,"abstract":"Presents personalized email prioritization in which importance is learned separately for each user's mailbox. User-specific social-network features and semi-supervised propagation of importance labels are combined with conventional message features to predict personal importance levels.","venue":"KDD 2009"}
{"title":"Real-time decision detection in multi-party dialogue","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D09-1118/","doi":"10.3115/1699648.1699659","authors":["Matthew Frampton","Jia Huang","Trung Huu Bui","Stanley Peters"],"year":2009,"published":"2009-08-06","abstract":"Presents real-time detection of decision-making sub-dialogues in multi-party meetings by distinguishing utterances according to their role in forming a decision. The work targets immediate extraction of evolving decision information from collaborative communication.","venue":"EMNLP 2009"}
{"title":"Robust Estimation of Message Importance Using Inferred Inter-recipient Trust for Supporting Email Triage","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SAINT.2010.53","doi":"10.1109/SAINT.2010.53","authors":["Sho Tsugawa","Kazuya Takahashi","Hiroyuki Ohsaki","Makoto Imase"],"year":2010,"abstract":"Extends trust-based email triage to estimate the importance of individual messages using inferred trust relationships between recipients. The method is evaluated on the Enron corpus as a mechanism for robust priority estimation.","venue":"IEEE/IPSJ International Symposium on Applications and the Internet 2010"}
{"title":"Modeling personalized email prioritization: Classification-based and regression-based approaches","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/2063576.2063683","doi":"10.1145/2063576.2063683","authors":["Shinjae Yoo","Yiming Yang","Jaime G. Carbonell"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Studies user-specific priority modeling for incoming email so that important messages can be processed ahead of lower-priority material. It compares classification and ordinal-regression approaches to predicting personalized priority levels.","venue":"CIKM 2011"}
{"title":"Summarize What You Are Interested In: An Optimization Framework for Interactive Personalized Summarization","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D11-1124/","authors":["Rui Yan","Jian-Yun Nie","Xiaoming Li"],"year":2011,"abstract":"Introduces interactive personalized summarization in which a summary and the model of user interests can be adapted through user interaction rather than producing the same static summary for every user. The optimization framework incorporates feedback about what information a user wants represented.","venue":"EMNLP 2011"}
{"title":"Estimating Message Importance Using Inferred Inter-recipient Trust for Supporting Email Triage","url":"https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/ipsjjip/20/3/20_796/_article","doi":"10.2197/ipsjjip.20.796","authors":["Sho Tsugawa","Hiroyuki Ohsaki","Makoto Imase"],"year":2012,"published":"2012-07-15","abstract":"Proposes message-level email prioritization using trust inferred from relationships among recipients. The EMIRT method is evaluated on email datasets as support for triage and is designed to reduce the risk of assigning low priority to messages needing urgent attention.","venue":"Journal of Information Processing"}
{"title":"Annotation and Classification of an Email Importance Corpus","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P15-2107/","doi":"10.3115/v1/P15-2107","authors":["Fan Zhang","Kui Xu"],"year":2015,"abstract":"Constructs and annotates a corpus for email importance and investigates automatic importance classification. The study examines importance in organizational email, including differences associated with organizational hierarchy.","venue":"ACL-IJCNLP 2015"}
{"title":"Assessing e-mail intent and tasks in e-mail messages","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0020025516301438","doi":"10.1016/j.ins.2016.03.002","authors":["Maya Sappelli","Gabriella Pasi","Suzan Verberne","Maaike de Boer","Wessel Kraaij"],"year":2016,"published":"2016-09-01","abstract":"Analyzes corporate email as a medium for conveying work tasks and proposes a taxonomy for task- and intent-based categorization. The annotations cover dimensions including whether a reply is expected, how many tasks occur, and temporal or spatial sensitivity, which can support more detailed priority estimation.","venue":"Information Sciences"}
{"title":"Exploring Email Triage: Challenges and Opportunities","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3295750.3298960","doi":"10.1145/3295750.3298960","authors":["Bahareh Sarrafzadeh","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Milad Shokouhi"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Uses interviews and survey data to study how people triage unhandled email and decide what to read, act on, or defer. The work characterizes user needs and challenges surrounding email attention and task handling.","venue":"CHIIR 2019"}
{"title":"Domain Adaptation for Commitment Detection in Email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3289600.3290984","doi":"10.1145/3289600.3290984","authors":["Hosein Azarbonyad","Robert Sim","Ryen W. White"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Studies detection of commitments expressed in email, where a sender promises future action that may warrant later reminder or tracking. Domain-adaptation methods are evaluated across different email corpora to reduce dependence on corpus-specific patterns.","venue":"WSDM 2019"}
{"title":"Summary Cloze: A New Task for Content Selection in Topic-Focused Summarization","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D19-1386/","doi":"10.18653/v1/D19-1386","authors":["Daniel Deutsch","Dan Roth"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Defines a sequential content-selection task in which the next summary sentence must be generated given a topic, reference documents, and the already written beginning of the summary. Conditioning on the partial summary explicitly exposes what information has already been accepted when deciding what to include next.","venue":"EMNLP-IJCNLP 2019"}
{"title":"Decision-Focused Summarization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06896","arxiv_id":"2109.06896","authors":["Chao-Chun Hsu","Chenhao Tan"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Studies summarization whose content is selected for usefulness to a particular downstream decision rather than generic salience alone. The framework selects representative information while accounting for non-redundancy and preservation of decision-relevant evidence.","venue":"EMNLP 2021"}
{"title":"EmailSum: Abstractive Email Thread Summarization","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.14691","arxiv_id":"2107.14691","authors":["Shiyue Zhang","Asli Celikyilmaz","Jianfeng Gao","Mohit Bansal"],"year":2021,"published":"2021-07-30","abstract":"Introduces a dataset and models for abstractive summarization of multi-message email threads. The work highlights email-specific challenges such as understanding sender intent and participant roles and evaluates both short and long thread summaries.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"Measurement of Perceived Importance and Urgency of Email: An Employees’ Perspective","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jcmc/article/27/2/zmac001/6548841","doi":"10.1093/jcmc/zmac001","authors":["Andre Lanctot","Linda Duxbury"],"year":2022,"published":"2022-03-15","abstract":"Develops and validates employee-centered measures distinguishing important email from email perceived as both important and urgent. The study identifies constructs useful for modeling workplace priority rather than equating priority with simple reply behavior or message frequency.","venue":"Journal of Computer-Mediated Communication"}
{"title":"PDSum: Prototype-driven Continuous Summarization of Evolving Multi-document Sets Stream","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.05550","arxiv_id":"2302.05550","doi":"10.1145/3543507.3583371","authors":["Susik Yoon","Hou Pong Chan","Jiawei Han"],"year":2023,"abstract":"Defines continuous summarization for dynamically evolving multi-document sets. PDSum maintains lightweight prototypes that adapt to newly arriving documents while preserving accumulated knowledge from previous documents, and updates summaries using relevance, novelty, and distinctiveness.","venue":"The Web Conference 2023"}
{"title":"Meeting Action Item Detection with Regularized Context Modeling","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.16763","arxiv_id":"2303.16763","authors":["Jiaqing Liu","Chong Deng","Qinglin Zhang","Qian Chen","Wen Wang"],"year":2023,"published":"2023-03-27","abstract":"Studies detection of meeting content associated with action items, treating action items as post-meeting tasks. The method models local and global conversational context to identify content that should remain operationally salient after the discussion.","venue":"arXiv"}
{"title":"AI-Powered Reminders for Collaborative Tasks: Experiences and Futures","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.01365","arxiv_id":"2403.01365","doi":"10.1145/3653701","authors":["Katelyn Morrison","Shamsi T. Iqbal","Eric Horvitz"],"year":2024,"published":"2024-04-26","abstract":"Studies knowledge workers' experiences with Microsoft's Viva Daily Briefing Email, which uses machine learning to surface commitments and requests embedded in unstructured email as reminders. Interviews and surveys examine what users want to be reminded about, how reminders fit workflows, and how perceived value varies with work style.","venue":"Proceedings of the ACM on Human-Computer Interaction"}
